186045417] [17592186080934 #edb/ref 1080 "(3,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080934 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080934 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6374228-908a-5ae3-9c45-b9246f1a198a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080935 #edb/ref 1080 "(-3, 1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080935 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080935 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d99a2c95-c164-5754-a9b1-a1a52d2e03fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080936 #edb/ref 1080 "(-3,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080936 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080936 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ecaaa766-bd6d-598a-9148-aa3b41482c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080937 #edb/ref 1080 "(3, 1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080937 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080937 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f74c14b0-895a-5b9f-afb2-01faa8afc6da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080938 #edb/ref 1080 "(1, 3)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3183da3e-0d0c-5e53-9bb2-7ed0745d4749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082977 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082977 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n![](images/99/99f02265f84874638b3faae106bb293e238b5e5064ea09beb2a6d71aeff12fe5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad4ecf44-6f6e-51a8-9c37-57483f958c72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082977 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that each point $(a,b)$ on $y = f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on\n\n$$\ny = f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nIn other words, the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y=f^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(0, 1) & ⟶(1, 0) \\\\ (-2, 5) & ⟶(5,-2)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo graph\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nwe plot the two points $(1,0)$ and $(5,-2)$ and connect the segment. We see that, as expected, the function\n\n$$\ny = f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nis a reflection of $y = f(x)$ in the line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/e7/e701f7e4f82b2ae3f6e48abcbbce350f57d6f54f9d4a98c03eabeaec89a328dd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082978 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082978 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(2,9)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x)$, which point must lie on the graph of $y=f^{-1}(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31a829fb-738f-579d-9232-7313c531733a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082978 #edb/ref 1074 "The function\n\n$$\ny = f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nis a reflection of $y = f(x)$ in the line $y=x$. Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y = f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on\n\n$$\ny = f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nIn other words, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nSince the point $(2,9)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, the point $(9,2)$ lies on\n\n$$\ny = f^{-1}(x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083160 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Graph of an Inverse Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083160 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083160 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "321d88fd-fca2-5907-8a16-809b3795c640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Points That Lie on the Graph on an Inverse Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083161 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "64cd9663-3e98-5508-9279-9af971ab13b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1045 "Graphs of Inverse Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083446 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e7e95ebe-a6d4-54ce-af5e-ef12ac0f54c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083666 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083666 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/46022df1ffb1b45ffe344658075dd363f3e2856798050fdbe85aacff0bc98ebe.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61beea40-a442-5d88-bc28-c86f6bb11eea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083667 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on the graph $y = f(x)$, which point must lie on the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4bff519-79b1-5c4d-b25d-261bafc7dc6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084010 #edb/ref 1061 13792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f462406-aa92-557b-8726-d65ca3ece754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084011 #edb/ref 1061 13795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084011 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c915c7b8-4ebd-5772-a1b7-fe38b1c8862e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084011 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084012 #edb/ref 1061 13801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "292083a0-53fe-58ee-833c-f83407811012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084421 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f(x)} = 4x-2\n$$\n\ndefined over the entire real line, and its inverse function\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f^{-1}(x)} = \\dfrac{1}{4}(x+2)\n$$\n\nIf we plot both\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\non the same diagram, we can see that the graph of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\nis a **reflection** of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nin the line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/ec/ec501e8c71fdcc1cfdd78e47ed81542bbba44aa8ac50084cc6ddfeb7eff011ff.png)\n\nEach point $(a,b)$ on the graph of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nhas a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\nThat is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nFor example, notice that the point $A(2,6)$ on\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nhas the corresponding point $A'(6,2)$ on\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084421 #edb/ref 1065 2764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084421 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6b66591-b35b-5dff-9df3-0030f51e2811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084421 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
